steps {
  type: CORTEX_STEP_TYPE_USER_INPUT
  status: CORTEX_STEP_STATUS_DONE
  metadata {
    created_at {
      seconds: 1787415303
      nanos: 524539985
    }
    source: CORTEX_STEP_SOURCE_USER_EXPLICIT
  }
  user_input {
    user_response: "I already have an existing application called \"NovaPath\".\n\nIMPORTANT:\nDO NOT rebuild the application from scratch.\n\nModify and extend the EXISTING NovaPath codebase while preserving its current functionality and design wherever possible.\n\nNovaPath is a LaunchPadX 2026 Track 01 \342\200\224 Agent Hub project.\n\nThe application must demonstrate:\n- Multi-step reasoning\n- Task decomposition\n- External API/web interaction\n- Persistent user/profile memory\n- Database retrieval\n- Minimal human input\n- Agentic workflow rather than a stateless chatbot\n\nI now want to add Firebase Authentication, Cloud Firestore, and Gemini API integration.\n\n==================================================\nPART 1 \342\200\224 DO NOT BREAK THE EXISTING APPLICATION\n==================================================\n\nBefore making changes:\n\n1. Inspect the existing codebase.\n2. Understand the current frontend.\n3. Identify the current agent workflow.\n4. Identify existing API/AI integrations.\n5. Identify existing components and routes.\n6. Reuse existing components wherever possible.\n7. Do not unnecessarily rename files, components, variables, or routes.\n8. Do not remove existing working functionality unless explicitly required.\n9. Do not redesign the entire UI.\n\nMake the smallest necessary changes to add the requested functionality.\n\n==================================================\nPART 2 \342\200\224 LOGIN / SIGNUP\n==================================================\n\nCurrently there is NO login popup or login screen.\n\nAdd a proper authentication experience.\n\nWhen an unauthenticated user opens NovaPath:\n\nDisplay a clean login/signup screen before allowing access to the main dashboard.\n\nAuthentication should use Firebase Authentication.\n\nPrimary authentication method:\n\n- Google Sign-In\n\nIf practical, also support:\n\n- Email/password signup\n- Email/password login\n\nThe login experience should be simple and professional.\n\nAfter successful authentication:\n\nLogin screen\n        \342\206\223\nMain NovaPath dashboard\n\nThe user should NOT have to manually enter a user ID.\n\nUse the Firebase authenticated user\'s UID.\n\nShow the logged-in user\'s name/profile information in the application.\n\nAdd:\n\n- Logout\n- Authentication loading state\n- Authentication error handling\n- Protected application state\n\nDo not display the dashboard to an unauthenticated user.\n\n==================================================\nPART 3 \342\200\224 FIREBASE SETUP\n==================================================\n\nUse:\n\nFirebase Authentication\n+\nCloud Firestore\n\nDo not add unnecessary Firebase services.\n\nCreate a clean Firebase abstraction.\n\nRecommended structure:\n\nsrc/\n  firebase/\n    config\n    auth\n    firestore\n\n  services/\n    profileService\n    memoryService\n    opportunityService\n    agentRunService\n\nDo not put Firebase database calls directly inside every UI component.\n\n==================================================\nPART 4 \342\200\224 FIRESTORE DATABASE\n==================================================\n\nUse this structure:\n\nusers/{userId}/profile/data\n\nusers/{userId}/memories/{memoryId}\n\nusers/{userId}/agentRuns/{runId}\n\nusers/{userId}/savedOpportunities/{opportunityId}\n\nopportunities/{opportunityId}\n\n==================================================\nPART 5 \342\200\224 USER PROFILE\n==================================================\n\nStore:\n\n- name\n- email\n- degree\n- branch\n- year\n- location\n- skills\n- career interests\n- preferred opportunity type\n- remote preference\n- createdAt\n- updatedAt\n\nThe profile must persist across:\n\n- Page refresh\n- Logout/login\n- New browser session\n\nDo not rely only on React state or localStorage.\n\nFirestore must be the source of truth.\n\n==================================================\nPART 6 \342\200\224 PERSISTENT MEMORY\n==================================================\n\nPersistent memory is a CORE REQUIREMENT of NovaPath.\n\nExample:\n\nUser says:\n\n\"I prefer Hyderabad and remote AI/ML internships.\"\n\nThe agent should identify this as a useful preference and save it to:\n\nusers/{userId}/memories/{memoryId}\n\nExample:\n\n{\n  type: \"preference\",\n  content: \"User prefers Hyderabad and remote AI/ML internships.\",\n  importance: \"high\",\n  createdAt: ...\n}\n\nDuring a future agent run:\n\nUser:\n\"Find opportunities for me.\"\n\nNovaPath should:\n\n1. Load the authenticated user\'s profile.\n2. Retrieve relevant memories from Firestore.\n3. Use those memories during reasoning.\n4. Show the user that the preferences were remembered.\n\nExample UI:\n\n\"Using your saved preferences\"\n\n\342\234\223 Hyderabad\n\342\234\223 Remote\n\342\234\223 AI/ML\n\nThis information MUST come from Firestore.\n\nDo not hardcode these values.\n\n==================================================\nPART 7 \342\200\224 GEMINI API\n==================================================\n\nConnect NovaPath to the Gemini API for the AI reasoning components.\n\nUse the Gemini API for tasks such as:\n\n- Understanding the user\'s career goal\n- Breaking the goal into tasks\n- Reasoning over profile information\n- Explaining opportunity matches\n- Identifying skill gaps\n- Generating personalized action plans\n- Summarizing agent results\n\nIMPORTANT SECURITY REQUIREMENT:\n\nDO NOT hardcode the Gemini API key into frontend source code.\n\nDO NOT commit the API key to GitHub.\n\nUse the appropriate environment variable / secret mechanism supported by this project.\n\nCreate an example environment configuration such as:\n\nGEMINI_API_KEY=your_key_here\n\nBut never place the real API key into source-controlled files.\n\nIf the current application already has Gemini integration, inspect it first and reuse/refactor it rather than creating a duplicate integration.\n\n==================================================\nPART 8 \342\200\224 GEMINI SHOULD NOT CONTROL EVERYTHING\n==================================================\n\nDo NOT use Gemini for deterministic operations that can be handled by normal application code.\n\nFor example:\n\nProfile retrieval\n\342\206\222 Firestore\n\nMemory retrieval\n\342\206\222 Firestore\n\nAuthentication\n\342\206\222 Firebase Auth\n\nSaving opportunities\n\342\206\222 Firestore\n\nMatch score calculation\n\342\206\222 deterministic application logic\n\nGemini:\n\342\206\222 reasoning\n\342\206\222 decomposition\n\342\206\222 explanation\n\342\206\222 skill-gap analysis\n\342\206\222 action-plan generation\n\nThis keeps the system reliable and easier to debug.\n\n==================================================\nPART 9 \342\200\224 AGENT WORKFLOW\n==================================================\n\nNovaPath must NOT behave as a simple:\n\nUser prompt \342\206\222 Gemini response\n\nInstead implement this workflow:\n\nUSER GOAL\n    \342\206\223\nAuthenticate user\n    \342\206\223\nLoad Firebase profile\n    \342\206\223\nRetrieve relevant memories\n    \342\206\223\nGemini understands goal\n    \342\206\223\nTask decomposition\n    \342\206\223\nExternal opportunity search/API\n    \342\206\223\nRetrieve opportunity data\n    \342\206\223\nDeterministic opportunity matching\n    \342\206\223\nGemini explains matches\n    \342\206\223\nSkill-gap analysis\n    \342\206\223\nPersonalized action plan\n    \342\206\223\nSave agent run\n    \342\206\223\nSave useful new memory\n    \342\206\223\nDisplay final result\n\nThe UI should make the multi-step process visible.\n\nExample:\n\n\342\234\223 Profile loaded\n\342\234\223 Preferences retrieved\n\342\234\223 Goal analyzed\n\342\234\223 Goal decomposed\n\342\234\223 Opportunities searched\n\342\234\223 Opportunities evaluated\n\342\234\223 Skill gaps identified\n\342\234\223 Action plan created\n\n==================================================\nPART 10 \342\200\224 OPPORTUNITY DATA\n==================================================\n\nKeep the existing opportunity data implementation if it already works.\n\nIf the current project uses demo/mock opportunities:\n\nDO NOT pretend they are live opportunities.\n\nClearly separate:\n\nDemo data\nfrom\nLive external opportunity data.\n\nCreate a modular opportunity service so that a real API can be connected later.\n\nDo not fabricate:\n\n- deadlines\n- organizations\n- eligibility\n- URLs\n- application status\n\n==================================================\nPART 11 \342\200\224 MATCHING\n==================================================\n\nUse a transparent deterministic scoring system.\n\nSuggested:\n\nSkill match: 40%\nEligibility: 25%\nLocation: 15%\nOpportunity preference: 10%\nExperience/year fit: 10%\n\nGemini can explain WHY an opportunity is a good match.\n\nGemini should NOT arbitrarily invent the numerical score.\n\nDisplay:\n\nMatch Score: 91%\n\nReasons:\n\342\234\223 Strong Python/ML skill match\n\342\234\223 Hyderabad preference matched\n\342\234\223 Suitable for second-year student\n\nSkill gaps:\n\342\226\263 TensorFlow\n\342\226\263 Docker\n\n==================================================\nPART 12 \342\200\224 AGENT RUN HISTORY\n==================================================\n\nSave each completed agent execution to:\n\nusers/{userId}/agentRuns/{runId}\n\nStore:\n\n- goal\n- status\n- steps\n- summary\n- createdAt\n- completedAt\n\nAllow the user to see previous agent runs.\n\n==================================================\nPART 13 \342\200\224 SAVED OPPORTUNITIES\n==================================================\n\nAllow users to save opportunities.\n\nStore them under:\n\nusers/{userId}/savedOpportunities/{opportunityId}\n\nInclude:\n\n- title\n- organization\n- source\n- URL\n- matchScore\n- savedAt\n- applicationStatus\n\n==================================================\nPART 14 \342\200\224 FIRESTORE SECURITY\n==================================================\n\nCreate secure Firestore rules.\n\nUsers can only access their own:\n\n- profile\n- memories\n- agent runs\n- saved opportunities\n\nUser A must NEVER be able to read User B\'s private data.\n\nDo NOT use:\n\nallow read, write: if true;\n\nUse authenticated user UID checks.\n\n==================================================\nPART 15 \342\200\224 UI FLEXIBILITY\n==================================================\n\nI will make additional UI changes after Firebase integration.\n\nTherefore:\n\nDO NOT tightly couple the database logic to the UI.\n\nCreate reusable service functions such as:\n\ngetUserProfile()\nupdateUserProfile()\nsaveMemory()\ngetMemories()\nsaveAgentRun()\ngetAgentRuns()\nsaveOpportunity()\ngetSavedOpportunities()\n\nThe UI should call these services.\n\nThis will allow me to:\n\n- Add UI sections\n- Remove UI sections\n- Change dashboard layout\n- Change cards\n- Change navigation\n- Add new screens\n\nwithout rewriting the Firebase logic.\n\n==================================================\nPART 16 \342\200\224 FUTURE UI CHANGES\n==================================================\n\nDo not assume the current UI is final.\n\nKeep the following modular:\n\n- Dashboard\n- Profile\n- Memory\n- Opportunity cards\n- Agent progress\n- Agent results\n- Saved opportunities\n- Agent history\n- Settings\n\nDo not duplicate UI logic.\n\n==================================================\nPART 17 \342\200\224 ERROR HANDLING\n==================================================\n\nHandle:\n\n- Firebase login failure\n- Firestore failure\n- Gemini API failure\n- External API failure\n- Empty profile\n- Empty memory\n- Invalid user input\n- Network failure\n- API timeout\n\nThe application must not crash.\n\nShow useful messages.\n\nExample:\n\n\"NovaPath couldn\'t retrieve opportunities right now. Your profile and saved data are safe. Please try again.\"\n\n==================================================\nPART 18 \342\200\224 DEMO MODE / BACKUP\n==================================================\n\nCreate a reliable fallback mechanism for the hackathon.\n\nIf the external opportunity API fails:\n\nUse clearly labelled demo opportunity data.\n\nIf Gemini fails:\n\nDisplay a graceful error and allow the demo dataset to remain accessible.\n\nDo NOT fake a successful API response while claiming it came from a live API.\n\nThe demo should remain functional even if one external dependency fails.\n\n==================================================\nPART 19 \342\200\224 HACKATHON DEMO\n==================================================\n\nThe following exact scenario must work:\n\nSTEP 1:\n\nUser logs in with Google.\n\nSTEP 2:\n\nUser profile contains:\n\nB.Tech\nAI/ML\n2nd year\nHyderabad\nPython\nMachine Learning\n\nSTEP 3:\n\nUser says:\n\n\"I prefer Hyderabad and remote AI/ML internships.\"\n\nNovaPath saves this preference.\n\nSTEP 4:\n\nRefresh the browser.\n\nSTEP 5:\n\nUser starts a new session.\n\nUser says:\n\n\"Find opportunities for me.\"\n\nSTEP 6:\n\nNovaPath retrieves the profile and memory from Firestore.\n\nDisplay:\n\n\"Welcome back. I remembered your preferences.\"\n\nSTEP 7:\n\nAgent executes multiple steps.\n\nSTEP 8:\n\nShow ranked opportunities.\n\nSTEP 9:\n\nShow skill gaps.\n\nSTEP 10:\n\nShow personalized action plan.\n\nSTEP 11:\n\nSave the agent run to Firestore.\n\nThis is the primary hackathon demonstration.\n\n==================================================\nPART 20 \342\200\224 CODE QUALITY\n==================================================\n\nKeep the implementation beginner-friendly.\n\nAvoid:\n\n- unnecessary microservices\n- complex infrastructure\n- unnecessary libraries\n- unnecessary abstractions\n- complicated state-management systems\n- vector databases unless genuinely required\n\nPrefer simple, readable TypeScript/JavaScript.\n\n==================================================\nPART 21 \342\200\224 ANTIGRAVITY COMPATIBILITY\n==================================================\n\nI will later move this codebase to Google Antigravity IDE.\n\nTherefore:\n\n- Keep standard project structure.\n- Keep Firebase configuration modular.\n- Keep Gemini integration modular.\n- Keep environment variables documented.\n- Keep services separated from UI.\n- Do not use proprietary abstractions that make migration difficult.\n- Create/update README with setup instructions.\n\nInclude:\n\n1. Firebase setup instructions\n2. Firestore database structure\n3. Authentication setup\n4. Gemini API setup\n5. Environment variables\n6. Local development instructions\n7. Deployment instructions\n8. Security rules\n\n==================================================\nPART 22 \342\200\224 FINAL VERIFICATION\n==================================================\n\nBefore finishing, verify the actual application.\n\nTest:\n\n\342\234\223 Login works\n\342\234\223 Logout works\n\342\234\223 Auth state persists\n\342\234\223 Profile saves\n\342\234\223 Profile loads after refresh\n\342\234\223 Memory saves\n\342\234\223 Memory loads in a new session\n\342\234\223 Agent performs multiple steps\n\342\234\223 Gemini responds correctly\n\342\234\223 Opportunity data loads\n\342\234\223 Match scoring works\n\342\234\223 Skill gaps are generated\n\342\234\223 Action plan is generated\n\342\234\223 Agent run is saved\n\342\234\223 Saved opportunity works\n\342\234\223 Firestore security works\n\342\234\223 API failures are handled\n\342\234\223 No API keys are exposed\n\342\234\223 Existing UI functionality still works\n\nIMPORTANT:\n\nDo not simply tell me that these features were implemented.\n\nActually inspect and test the application.\n\nAt the end, provide a concise summary of:\n\n1. Files changed\n2. Firebase services added\n3. Firestore collections created\n4. Gemini integration\n5. Environment variables required\n6. Security rules\n7. Tests performed\n8. Any remaining issues"
    items {
      text: "I already have an existing application called \"NovaPath\".\n\nIMPORTANT:\nDO NOT rebuild the application from scratch.\n\nModify and extend the EXISTING NovaPath codebase while preserving its current functionality and design wherever possible.\n\nNovaPath is a LaunchPadX 2026 Track 01 \342\200\224 Agent Hub project.\n\nThe application must demonstrate:\n- Multi-step reasoning\n- Task decomposition\n- External API/web interaction\n- Persistent user/profile memory\n- Database retrieval\n- Minimal human input\n- Agentic workflow rather than a stateless chatbot\n\nI now want to add Firebase Authentication, Cloud Firestore, and Gemini API integration.\n\n==================================================\nPART 1 \342\200\224 DO NOT BREAK THE EXISTING APPLICATION\n==================================================\n\nBefore making changes:\n\n1. Inspect the existing codebase.\n2. Understand the current frontend.\n3. Identify the current agent workflow.\n4. Identify existing API/AI integrations.\n5. Identify existing components and routes.\n6. Reuse existing components wherever possible.\n7. Do not unnecessarily rename files, components, variables, or routes.\n8. Do not remove existing working functionality unless explicitly required.\n9. Do not redesign the entire UI.\n\nMake the smallest necessary changes to add the requested functionality.\n\n==================================================\nPART 2 \342\200\224 LOGIN / SIGNUP\n==================================================\n\nCurrently there is NO login popup or login screen.\n\nAdd a proper authentication experience.\n\nWhen an unauthenticated user opens NovaPath:\n\nDisplay a clean login/signup screen before allowing access to the main dashboard.\n\nAuthentication should use Firebase Authentication.\n\nPrimary authentication method:\n\n- Google Sign-In\n\nIf practical, also support:\n\n- Email/password signup\n- Email/password login\n\nThe login experience should be simple and professional.\n\nAfter successful authentication:\n\nLogin screen\n        \342\206\223\nMain NovaPath dashboard\n\nThe user should NOT have to manually enter a user ID.\n\nUse the Firebase authenticated user\'s UID.\n\nShow the logged-in user\'s name/profile information in the application.\n\nAdd:\n\n- Logout\n- Authentication loading state\n- Authentication error handling\n- Protected application state\n\nDo not display the dashboard to an unauthenticated user.\n\n==================================================\nPART 3 \342\200\224 FIREBASE SETUP\n==================================================\n\nUse:\n\nFirebase Authentication\n+\nCloud Firestore\n\nDo not add unnecessary Firebase services.\n\nCreate a clean Firebase abstraction.\n\nRecommended structure:\n\nsrc/\n  firebase/\n    config\n    auth\n    firestore\n\n  services/\n    profileService\n    memoryService\n    opportunityService\n    agentRunService\n\nDo not put Firebase database calls directly inside every UI component.\n\n==================================================\nPART 4 \342\200\224 FIRESTORE DATABASE\n==================================================\n\nUse this structure:\n\nusers/{userId}/profile/data\n\nusers/{userId}/memories/{memoryId}\n\nusers/{userId}/agentRuns/{runId}\n\nusers/{userId}/savedOpportunities/{opportunityId}\n\nopportunities/{opportunityId}\n\n==================================================\nPART 5 \342\200\224 USER PROFILE\n==================================================\n\nStore:\n\n- name\n- email\n- degree\n- branch\n- year\n- location\n- skills\n- career interests\n- preferred opportunity type\n- remote preference\n- createdAt\n- updatedAt\n\nThe profile must persist across:\n\n- Page refresh\n- Logout/login\n- New browser session\n\nDo not rely only on React state or localStorage.\n\nFirestore must be the source of truth.\n\n==================================================\nPART 6 \342\200\224 PERSISTENT MEMORY\n==================================================\n\nPersistent memory is a CORE REQUIREMENT of NovaPath.\n\nExample:\n\nUser says:\n\n\"I prefer Hyderabad and remote AI/ML internships.\"\n\nThe agent should identify this as a useful preference and save it to:\n\nusers/{userId}/memories/{memoryId}\n\nExample:\n\n{\n  type: \"preference\",\n  content: \"User prefers Hyderabad and remote AI/ML internships.\",\n  importance: \"high\",\n  createdAt: ...\n}\n\nDuring a future agent run:\n\nUser:\n\"Find opportunities for me.\"\n\nNovaPath should:\n\n1. Load the authenticated user\'s profile.\n2. Retrieve relevant memories from Firestore.\n3. Use those memories during reasoning.\n4. Show the user that the preferences were remembered.\n\nExample UI:\n\n\"Using your saved preferences\"\n\n\342\234\223 Hyderabad\n\342\234\223 Remote\n\342\234\223 AI/ML\n\nThis information MUST come from Firestore.\n\nDo not hardcode these values.\n\n==================================================\nPART 7 \342\200\224 GEMINI API\n==================================================\n\nConnect NovaPath to the Gemini API for the AI reasoning components.\n\nUse the Gemini API for tasks such as:\n\n- Understanding the user\'s career goal\n- Breaking the goal into tasks\n- Reasoning over profile information\n- Explaining opportunity matches\n- Identifying skill gaps\n- Generating personalized action plans\n- Summarizing agent results\n\nIMPORTANT SECURITY REQUIREMENT:\n\nDO NOT hardcode the Gemini API key into frontend source code.\n\nDO NOT commit the API key to GitHub.\n\nUse the appropriate environment variable / secret mechanism supported by this project.\n\nCreate an example environment configuration such as:\n\nGEMINI_API_KEY=your_key_here\n\nBut never place the real API key into source-controlled files.\n\nIf the current application already has Gemini integration, inspect it first and reuse/refactor it rather than creating a duplicate integration.\n\n==================================================\nPART 8 \342\200\224 GEMINI SHOULD NOT CONTROL EVERYTHING\n==================================================\n\nDo NOT use Gemini for deterministic operations that can be handled by normal application code.\n\nFor example:\n\nProfile retrieval\n\342\206\222 Firestore\n\nMemory retrieval\n\342\206\222 Firestore\n\nAuthentication\n\342\206\222 Firebase Auth\n\nSaving opportunities\n\342\206\222 Firestore\n\nMatch score calculation\n\342\206\222 deterministic application logic\n\nGemini:\n\342\206\222 reasoning\n\342\206\222 decomposition\n\342\206\222 explanation\n\342\206\222 skill-gap analysis\n\342\206\222 action-plan generation\n\nThis keeps the system reliable and easier to debug.\n\n==================================================\nPART 9 \342\200\224 AGENT WORKFLOW\n==================================================\n\nNovaPath must NOT behave as a simple:\n\nUser prompt \342\206\222 Gemini response\n\nInstead implement this workflow:\n\nUSER GOAL\n    \342\206\223\nAuthenticate user\n    \342\206\223\nLoad Firebase profile\n    \342\206\223\nRetrieve relevant memories\n    \342\206\223\nGemini understands goal\n    \342\206\223\nTask decomposition\n    \342\206\223\nExternal opportunity search/API\n    \342\206\223\nRetrieve opportunity data\n    \342\206\223\nDeterministic opportunity matching\n    \342\206\223\nGemini explains matches\n    \342\206\223\nSkill-gap analysis\n    \342\206\223\nPersonalized action plan\n    \342\206\223\nSave agent run\n    \342\206\223\nSave useful new memory\n    \342\206\223\nDisplay final result\n\nThe UI should make the multi-step process visible.\n\nExample:\n\n\342\234\223 Profile loaded\n\342\234\223 Preferences retrieved\n\342\234\223 Goal analyzed\n\342\234\223 Goal decomposed\n\342\234\223 Opportunities searched\n\342\234\223 Opportunities evaluated\n\342\234\223 Skill gaps identified\n\342\234\223 Action plan created\n\n==================================================\nPART 10 \342\200\224 OPPORTUNITY DATA\n==================================================\n\nKeep the existing opportunity data implementation if it already works.\n\nIf the current project uses demo/mock opportunities:\n\nDO NOT pretend they are live opportunities.\n\nClearly separate:\n\nDemo data\nfrom\nLive external opportunity data.\n\nCreate a modular opportunity service so that a real API can be connected later.\n\nDo not fabricate:\n\n- deadlines\n- organizations\n- eligibility\n- URLs\n- application status\n\n==================================================\nPART 11 \342\200\224 MATCHING\n==================================================\n\nUse a transparent deterministic scoring system.\n\nSuggested:\n\nSkill match: 40%\nEligibility: 25%\nLocation: 15%\nOpportunity preference: 10%\nExperience/year fit: 10%\n\nGemini can explain WHY an opportunity is a good match.\n\nGemini should NOT arbitrarily invent the numerical score.\n\nDisplay:\n\nMatch Score: 91%\n\nReasons:\n\342\234\223 Strong Python/ML skill match\n\342\234\223 Hyderabad preference matched\n\342\234\223 Suitable for second-year student\n\nSkill gaps:\n\342\226\263 TensorFlow\n\342\226\263 Docker\n\n==================================================\nPART 12 \342\200\224 AGENT RUN HISTORY\n==================================================\n\nSave each completed agent execution to:\n\nusers/{userId}/agentRuns/{runId}\n\nStore:\n\n- goal\n- status\n- steps\n- summary\n- createdAt\n- completedAt\n\nAllow the user to see previous agent runs.\n\n==================================================\nPART 13 \342\200\224 SAVED OPPORTUNITIES\n==================================================\n\nAllow users to save opportunities.\n\nStore them under:\n\nusers/{userId}/savedOpportunities/{opportunityId}\n\nInclude:\n\n- title\n- organization\n- source\n- URL\n- matchScore\n- savedAt\n- applicationStatus\n\n==================================================\nPART 14 \342\200\224 FIRESTORE SECURITY\n==================================================\n\nCreate secure Firestore rules.\n\nUsers can only access their own:\n\n- profile\n- memories\n- agent runs\n- saved opportunities\n\nUser A must NEVER be able to read User B\'s private data.\n\nDo NOT use:\n\nallow read, write: if true;\n\nUse authenticated user UID checks.\n\n==================================================\nPART 15 \342\200\224 UI FLEXIBILITY\n==================================================\n\nI will make additional UI changes after Firebase integration.\n\nTherefore:\n\nDO NOT tightly couple the database logic to the UI.\n\nCreate reusable service functions such as:\n\ngetUserProfile()\nupdateUserProfile()\nsaveMemory()\ngetMemories()\nsaveAgentRun()\ngetAgentRuns()\nsaveOpportunity()\ngetSavedOpportunities()\n\nThe UI should call these services.\n\nThis will allow me to:\n\n- Add UI sections\n- Remove UI sections\n- Change dashboard layout\n- Change cards\n- Change navigation\n- Add new screens\n\nwithout rewriting the Firebase logic.\n\n==================================================\nPART 16 \342\200\224 FUTURE UI CHANGES\n==================================================\n\nDo not assume the current UI is final.\n\nKeep the following modular:\n\n- Dashboard\n- Profile\n- Memory\n- Opportunity cards\n- Agent progress\n- Agent results\n- Saved opportunities\n- Agent history\n- Settings\n\nDo not duplicate UI logic.\n\n==================================================\nPART 17 \342\200\224 ERROR HANDLING\n==================================================\n\nHandle:\n\n- Firebase login failure\n- Firestore failure\n- Gemini API failure\n- External API failure\n- Empty profile\n- Empty memory\n- Invalid user input\n- Network failure\n- API timeout\n\nThe application must not crash.\n\nShow useful messages.\n\nExample:\n\n\"NovaPath couldn\'t retrieve opportunities right now. Your profile and saved data are safe. Please try again.\"\n\n==================================================\nPART 18 \342\200\224 DEMO MODE / BACKUP\n==================================================\n\nCreate a reliable fallback mechanism for the hackathon.\n\nIf the external opportunity API fails:\n\nUse clearly labelled demo opportunity data.\n\nIf Gemini fails:\n\nDisplay a graceful error and allow the demo dataset to remain accessible.\n\nDo NOT fake a successful API response while claiming it came from a live API.\n\nThe demo should remain functional even if one external dependency fails.\n\n==================================================\nPART 19 \342\200\224 HACKATHON DEMO\n==================================================\n\nThe following exact scenario must work:\n\nSTEP 1:\n\nUser logs in with Google.\n\nSTEP 2:\n\nUser profile contains:\n\nB.Tech\nAI/ML\n2nd year\nHyderabad\nPython\nMachine Learning\n\nSTEP 3:\n\nUser says:\n\n\"I prefer Hyderabad and remote AI/ML internships.\"\n\nNovaPath saves this preference.\n\nSTEP 4:\n\nRefresh the browser.\n\nSTEP 5:\n\nUser starts a new session.\n\nUser says:\n\n\"Find opportunities for me.\"\n\nSTEP 6:\n\nNovaPath retrieves the profile and memory from Firestore.\n\nDisplay:\n\n\"Welcome back. I remembered your preferences.\"\n\nSTEP 7:\n\nAgent executes multiple steps.\n\nSTEP 8:\n\nShow ranked opportunities.\n\nSTEP 9:\n\nShow skill gaps.\n\nSTEP 10:\n\nShow personalized action plan.\n\nSTEP 11:\n\nSave the agent run to Firestore.\n\nThis is the primary hackathon demonstration.\n\n==================================================\nPART 20 \342\200\224 CODE QUALITY\n==================================================\n\nKeep the implementation beginner-friendly.\n\nAvoid:\n\n- unnecessary microservices\n- complex infrastructure\n- unnecessary libraries\n- unnecessary abstractions\n- complicated state-management systems\n- vector databases unless genuinely required\n\nPrefer simple, readable TypeScript/JavaScript.\n\n==================================================\nPART 21 \342\200\224 ANTIGRAVITY COMPATIBILITY\n==================================================\n\nI will later move this codebase to Google Antigravity IDE.\n\nTherefore:\n\n- Keep standard project structure.\n- Keep Firebase configuration modular.\n- Keep Gemini integration modular.\n- Keep environment variables documented.\n- Keep services separated from UI.\n- Do not use proprietary abstractions that make migration difficult.\n- Create/update README with setup instructions.\n\nInclude:\n\n1. Firebase setup instructions\n2. Firestore database structure\n3. Authentication setup\n4. Gemini API setup\n5. Environment variables\n6. Local development instructions\n7. Deployment instructions\n8. Security rules\n\n==================================================\nPART 22 \342\200\224 FINAL VERIFICATION\n==================================================\n\nBefore finishing, verify the actual application.\n\nTest:\n\n\342\234\223 Login works\n\342\234\223 Logout works\n\342\234\223 Auth state persists\n\342\234\223 Profile saves\n\342\234\223 Profile loads after refresh\n\342\234\223 Memory saves\n\342\234\223 Memory loads in a new session\n\342\234\223 Agent performs multiple steps\n\342\234\223 Gemini responds correctly\n\342\234\223 Opportunity data loads\n\342\234\223 Match scoring works\n\342\234\223 Skill gaps are generated\n\342\234\223 Action plan is generated\n\342\234\223 Agent run is saved\n\342\234\223 Saved opportunity works\n\342\234\223 Firestore security works\n\342\234\223 API failures are handled\n\342\234\223 No API keys are exposed\n\342\234\223 Existing UI functionality still works\n\nIMPORTANT:\n\nDo not simply tell me that these features were implemented.\n\nActually inspect and test the application.\n\nAt the end, provide a concise summary of:\n\n1. Files changed\n2. Firebase services added\n3. Firestore collections created\n4. Gemini integration\n5. Environment variables required\n6. Security rules\n7. Tests performed\n8. Any remaining issues"
    }
  }
}
